        "id": "p23-q21-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$3x+y=5x+4$ and $3x+y=2y$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=4,\\ y=12$.",
          "worked": "Rearranging the second equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}3x+y&=2y\\\\y&=3x\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting into the first equation:\n$$\\begin{align*}3x+3x&=5x+4\\\\6x&=5x+4\\\\x&=4\\end{align*}$$\nThen\n$$\\begin{align*}y&=3\\times4\\\\y&=12\\end{align*}$$\nCheck: $3(4)+12=24=5(4)+4$ and $3(4)+12=24=2(12)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p23-q21-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$3x+2y=x-3y$ and $4x+6y=3x-7$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=5,\\ y=-2$.",
          "worked": "Rearranging gives\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+5y&=0\\qquad(1)\\\\x+6y&=-7\\qquad(2)\\end{align*}$$\nTwice equation (2) gives $2x+12y=-14$. Subtracting this from equation (1):\n$$\\begin{align*}(2x+5y)-(2x+12y)&=0-(-14)\\\\-7y&=14\\\\y&=-2\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting into equation (2):\n$$\\begin{align*}x+6(-2)&=-7\\\\x-12&=-7\\\\x&=5\\end{align*}$$\nCheck: $3(5)+2(-2)=11=5-3(-2)$ and $4(5)+6(-2)=8=3(5)-7$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      }
    ]
